{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1508040210,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUyNjU1NDQwMTc=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1902","origin":"github"},"title":"aggregate-metadata should have some --skip-aggregated","message":"#### What is the problem?\nOn a large hierarchy it is desired to update only pieces needed to be updated.  If I know that there were no changes in datalad, and metadata aggregation was already the last commit in a repo, there is no need to re-aggregate there.   Not sure if `--since` semantic kinda fits here... so may be `--skip-aggregated` would be more appropriate here... or may be skipping should be the default , and `--force` should be added to signal to re-aggregate even if meta-data aggregation was the last step.  May be it is worth storing datalad's version within aggregated meta-data so we could also trigger reaggregation automagically (and record new version) if new datalad is used\n\nedit 1: #850 originally requested `--since` for aggregate-metadata, so changing title to list only --skip aggregated","files":null}]}